      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure thing, let's break this down with a little bit of a \"goblin\" twist. So, when you're dealing with CIF (Cost, Insurance, and Freight) under Incoterms 2020, it's like you're handing over a treasure map to a goblin merchant. The goblin merchant is responsible for getting the treasure (your circuit boards) from Seoul to Hamburg, but only up to the port of Hamburg. Once the goblin merchant has safely delivered the treasure to the port, the risk of it getting goblin-gnawed or goblin-gobbled over to you, the buyer, passes over.\n\nNow, the goblin merchant must also provide you with the necessary insurance to cover the treasure's journey, just like a goblin would insure his precious loot against goblin thieves. The insurance should cover the cost of the circuit boards up to the port of Hamburg. So, in summary, the seller (goblin merchant) is responsible for the goods, the carriage, and the insurance until they reach the port of Hamburg. Once they do, the risk of goblin mischief (or any other mishap) passes over to you, the buyer. Just remember, if the goblin merchant fails to provide the",
